#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9818ae35-c8b3-5f88-ba2b-5d45142a8bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23+9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299254} {:answer/id #uuid "73906657-1425-51ce-a538-2aa005fbfb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299255} {:answer/id #uuid "47f2bfd2-8877-5c8d-baeb-95219fbfd2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299256} {:answer/id #uuid "83b79d39-0888-504f-8c23-2da1887a9f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+18\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299257} {:answer/id #uuid "87333285-38e3-5507-b35f-2d193c3ba5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87333285-38e3-5507-b35f-2d193c3ba5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299258} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e47631-c32b-5c32-9d8a-459bc3b07a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2db2de8c-b623-527a-a241-ca9def43988b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103771" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/68786f08e73998de597d0d9eea5be3031f322785c05835cf7eccfa7837f36d7b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/b553755a624031408b8374d06c8dc8dbf33c3ddc8d372332c945bad7e42ef068.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+(\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18)=\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8a8d6e-233e-5b48-8976-c06358afb546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73\\,225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299261} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d5695-9efa-5e4a-ab6b-7ea5f9ffed29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299262} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdb847b-1d92-5ccb-ba6b-56b98999ccef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89\\,850" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299263} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb86ed1-24f7-56df-a21e-7d7d67c11dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\,250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299264} {:answer/id #uuid "3637a9d0-2e3a-5d25-a194-e01b7625e8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3637a9d0-2e3a-5d25-a194-e01b7625e8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299265} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ddf580e-bb8e-58b9-8619-130ffdcec2ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de63944-9034-5911-a181-23fee9b4f403" :question/math-academy-id "q-103776" :question/problem "The bacteria culture in a lab increases at a rate of ${e}^{2t}$ per hour. If, after $2$ hours, there are $2\\,000$ bacteria in the population, approximately how many bacteria are in the population after $6$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "Let $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ be the rate at which the number of bacteria is changing and let $S(T)$ be the total number of bacteria and in the population after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+{\\int }_{2}^{T}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}{|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of bacteria in the culture after $T=6$ hours, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(6)=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{12}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}≈83\\,350.\n$$"}]}
